20:53:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're right, and "it barely matters on the world stage" isn't a controversial take at all—it's a pretty accurate description of the global consensus. I didn't mean to suggest otherwise. When I said "not insignificant," I was trying to draw a distinction between *global visibility* and *historical/regional relevance*, but I should have acknowledged your point more directly. Here's the reality: by almost any conventional measure of "mattering" in world affairs—economic leverage, military projection, diplomatic clout, cultural soft power, or even just headline frequency—Eritrea genuinely doesn't register for most people. That's not a media bias or a misunderstanding; it's a structural reality. The country has deliberately cut itself off from the international system, has a GDP roughly the size of a mid-sized Western city, and its government actively suppresses the kind of global engagement that generates influence. So when someone says "Eritrea is overlooked," the simplest answer is actually the one you've given: it's not overlooked, it's just *not that relevant* to most global conversations. It doesn't produce goods or ideas that the world consumes, it doesn't shape regional security in the way Ethiopia or Egypt do, and it's not a crisis hotspot that demands attention. In that sense, it's the definition of peripheral. What's interesting, though—and probably why researchers find it compelling—is *why* it ended up so marginal despite sitting on the Red Sea, with a fiercely independent history and a diaspora that sends back a billion dollars a year. That kind of self-imposed obscurity is rare, and in its own way, it makes Eritrea worth studying precisely because it's an outlier that's opted out of the game most nations are desperate to play. But yeah, if we're talking about raw global significance in the way people usually mean it, you're spot on. It doesn't move any needles, and nobody pretends it does.
